ans control  it now as we know  even things that are statistically  unlikely sometimes happen going into the  2016 presidential election the odds were  that Hillary Clinton had an 85 percent  chance of winning and Donald Trump had a  15 chance of winning now 85 percent is  not a hundred percent you run 100  simulations in 15 of them Trump would  come out the winner and indeed he did  this is a very very small margin but we  have to it's not even really come to  terms with it's understand that there's  a unique or at least an unlikely an  uncommon opportunity this November  normally and I know you guys or some of  you are sick of hearing this normally  when one party takes the White House in  the first midterm election they lose the  house and or senate or if they already  don't have control of the house they  fall even further behind or if they have  control of the house and retain it it's  by a smaller margin you get the point we  might not see that this November and if  the question is why which is to me the  most interesting question right now  everything we know everything we can say  is that it's because primarily of two  issues number one abortion and number  two gun safety although I have to say  compared to six eight weeks ago the gun  safety issue is not nearly in the  Forefront as much as it was but we have  two issues that normally are not good  issues for Democrats to run on that are  helping Democrats right now the country  MO mostly wants abortion to be legal in  most cases we saw that on Tuesday night  in Kansas  where Kansas voters voted to defeat an  amendment which would have allowed  completely Banning abortion they voted  to defeat that amendment by a 20-point  margin that is not a small margin and  that's a very red State one of the  reddest states in the country and when  Roe v Wade was overturned by the Supreme  Court even many centrists and  center-right people said that this is  wrong this is wrong abortion should be  legal in most cases and